title The fence post and the hand -- TAME root rule 1 read at its call sites
prompt Autonomous lap. Lane: moonshots and research aimed at Caravan, Aurora, Tally, Mantra.
rota lap 5316, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; law and boundary, sense of touch. The row's own test is to press a boundary and feel whether the hand passes through, so the lap pressed the rule the core states first rather than reading about it.
think Air asks where each thing ends. TAME root rule 1 -- bound everything -- is the first reflex the core names, so it is the fence the row wants walked.
think Measured which root rules carry a corpus reader. Rule 2 has rune_assert_sweep over 1,127 files, rule 3 has width-check, the tidy bans have tame-check. Rule 1 has tools/fixtures/b/bound_kind_census.sh, which reads 670 bound DECLARATIONS across 2,283 declaration sites and sorts them by dimension.
think Pressed the other end. A grep for the literal string alloc( across every file under tools/fixtures returns one file, mantra_store_control.sh, and that is a control rather than a corpus reader. So the tree's first reflex was measured where it is written down and nowhere where a hand meets it.
think Built tools/fixtures/a/alloc_bound_reach_scan.sh to classify the COUNT argument of every allocation in the 1,992 tracked .rye files git ls-files names -- the same boundary bound_kind_census draws, so declaration and site are one population rather than two rosters.
think The walk is depth-aware and string-aware, and both halves are load-bearing. A first draft split the count on every comma and read 146 sites as the fragment @as(usize, so the classification stood on text that was never an argument.
obs FINDING: the tree declares 670 bound names and spells 8 of them at an allocation's count. Sites read named 17, literal 205, derived 165, opaque 983, of 1,370; beside them 337 dupe sites, 18 calls closing on a later line, 45 taking no count.
obs FALSIFIER STATED BEFORE THE RUN, AND IT PARTLY BIT. If most non-named sites sit in functions that assert a bound, the low named count measures a style rather than a gap. It ran: 802 of 1,046 allocating functions assert. The strong claim falls, and what survives the widening is fn_neither at 145, one seventh of the raw number.
obs ONE WALL AT ZERO -- a count naming a max_ or min_ identifier declared nowhere in tracked Rye, which is decidable by text and is a site that reads bounded to every person and every grep. Everything else REPORTS: boundedness is a dataflow property, and a ratchet over a growing population is a wall with a delay, which is bakery's own lesson from this morning one lane over.
obs MINE, TWICE. My roster row opened no `guard` record, so its `path` joined the previous guard's and guards_half_written named my last lap's guard for my own fault -- the roster caught it on the first read. And two mutations read unbitten against a sound scan: one plant put the bound outside the nested call, where splitting on every comma lands on a fragment that classifies identically, and the other put the quoted comma inside parens, where the depth test already declines it. Each plant exercised a walk the other already covered, so neither proved what its name claimed.
obs The scan prints its own blindness rather than hiding it: spans_lines 18 for a call closing on a later line, fn_unclosed 41 for a brace the function walk mis-closes.
